+/*
+ * HEARTH: the app id carries the rules version, and is bumped by hand.
+ *
+ * Kangaroo installs the hApp once and never again: installHappIfNecessary
+ * returns early if this id is already installed. That is right for an app
+ * whose rules never change, and wrong for this one - shipping new rules under
+ * the same id would mean nobody ever runs them.
+ *
+ * So the id changes when, and only when, the integrity zome changes. The new
+ * version then installs alongside the old one, in the same conductor and the
+ * same keystore, and circles are carried across rather than stranded. See
+ * docs/upgrades.md in the Hearth repository.
+ *
+ * 'kangaroo.happ' is what Hearth 0.2.0 to 0.2.4 installed. It stays on disk
+ * holding the circles made under those rules, and must not be reused.
